# route — real-world examples

Legacy net-tools routing command. Modern replacement is `ip route` — equivalents
noted at the bottom.

## Show the routing table

```sh
# -n: numeric IPs instead of resolving host names (faster, clearer)
route -n
```

## Default gateway

```sh
# Add a default gateway
route add default gateway 192.168.0.1

# Add a default route via a named gateway host.
# The static route to "mango-gw" must exist first; the outgoing device
# is chosen by how mango-gw is reachable.
route add default gw mango-gw

# Delete the current default route
route del default
```

## Network routes

```sh
# Loopback entry, netmask 255.0.0.0 on device "lo"
route add -net 127.0.0.0 netmask 255.0.0.0 dev lo

# Route local network 192.56.76.x via eth0 ("dev" keyword optional)
route add -net 192.56.76.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 dev eth0

# Route a network through a SLIP host: first the host route, then the net
route add ipx4 sl0
route add -net 192.57.66.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 gw ipx4

# Rejecting route: block the private 10.x.x.x range
route add -net 10.0.0.0 netmask 255.0.0.0 reject

# All class D (multicast) routes via eth0 — normal config on a multicast kernel
route add -net 224.0.0.0 netmask 240.0.0.0 dev eth0
```

## Homelab examples

```sh
# Point traffic for the IoT VLAN at the router's second interface
route add -net 192.168.50.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 gw 192.168.1.254

# Modern `ip` equivalents (preferred on Tumbleweed):
ip route show                                   # route -n
ip route add default via 192.168.1.1            # route add default gw ...
ip route add 192.168.50.0/24 via 192.168.1.254  # route add -net ... gw ...
ip route del default                            # route del default
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-n` | Numeric addresses, no DNS lookups |
| `add` / `del` | Add or delete a route |
| `-net` / `-host` | Target is a network / single host |
| `netmask <mask>` | Netmask for the target net |
| `gw <addr>` | Route via gateway |
| `dev <iface>` | Force a device |
| `reject` | Install a blocking route |
